            "text": "McKinsey's \"Three Horizons of Growth\" framework (from Baghai, Coley, and\nWhite's 2000 book \"The Alchemy of Growth\") argues that a company should\nmanage three kinds of business simultaneously rather than judging its whole\nportfolio by one growth number. Horizon 1 is the core business that\nprovides the greatest current profit and cash flow -- the focus there is\ndefending and extracting maximum value through performance improvement.\nHorizon 2 is emerging, fast-growing lines that aren't yet the largest\nprofit contributor but could become Horizon-1-scale -- these need\ninvestment, not just monitoring. Horizon 3 is early-stage options for\nfuture growth. When analyzing a multi-segment business, this means a\nsegment that is small but accelerating (Horizon 2) should be judged on\ntrajectory and unit economics, not on its current share of company profit\n-- the mistake is applying Horizon-1 profitability standards to a\nHorizon-2 business and concluding it's underperforming.",
            "source": "McKinsey & Company, \"Enduring Ideas: The three horizons of growth\" -- https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/strategy-and-corporate-finance/our-insights/enduring-ideas-the-three-horizons-of-growth",
            "corpus": "consulting_best_practices",
            "score": 0.6462
          },
          {
            "text": "McKinsey's 2014 \"Grow Fast or Die Slow\" study examined thousands of software\nand technology companies from 1980-2012 and found that revenue growth rate\nis a far stronger predictor of long-term market-cap gain than margin\nimprovement, for companies under roughly $4 billion in revenue: growth\ncontributes roughly twice as much to market-cap gain as margin does at that\nscale. \"Supergrowers\" (50%+ CAGR) had materially lower EBITDA margins early\non (around 5% at $100M revenue) than slower growers, but that gap closes as\nthey scale past $1B in revenue (around 24% EBITDA margin). The practical\ntakeaway for reading a growth-stage company's numbers: a young, fast-growing\nsegment showing thin or negative margins is not automatically a red flag --\nit can be the expected, even desirable, shape of a company in its\nsupergrowth phase. The flag worth watching for is growth decelerating\nwithout margin improving to compensate -- that combination has no offsetting\nupside. Note the important exception: once a company passes roughly $4\nbillion in revenue, margin becomes the more important lever than growth.",
            "source": "McKinsey & Company, \"Grow fast or die slow: The role of profitability in sustainable growth\" -- https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/technology-media-and-telecommunications/our-insights/grow-fast-or-die-slow-the-role-of-profitability-in-sustainable-growth",
            "corpus": "consulting_best_practices",
