          },
          {
            "text": "BCG's Growth-Share Matrix (created by Bruce Henderson in 1968) classifies a\ncompany's business units along two axes -- relative market share and market\ngrowth rate -- into four categories. \"Stars\" (high growth, high share)\nwarrant continued heavy investment because they have high future potential.\n\"Cash cows\" (low growth, high share) generate more cash than they need and\nshould be harvested to fund growth elsewhere, not over-invested in. \"Question\nmarks\" (high growth, low share) are the ambiguous case -- worth investing in\nonly if there's a credible path to becoming a star, otherwise a candidate to\ndiscard. \"Dogs\" (low growth, low share) are candidates to divest or\nreposition. The strategic logic: market leadership in a category tends to\nproduce durable superior returns, and most business units move through this\ncycle (question mark -> star -> cash cow -> dog) as their market matures.\nApplied to a multi-segment company, the useful discipline is asking which\nquadrant each segment is actually in BEFORE judging it by the same yardstick\nas the others -- a fast-growing, lower-share segment (question mark/star\nterritory) should be evaluated on share-gain trajectory, not on the cash\ngeneration expected of a mature cash-cow segment.",
